  "slug": "long-zero-2365",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test how simulator, network, and generative model choices shape inference across epithelial, cancer, and genomic scales.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the computational model itself treated as a controllable variable in biological inference. PRISM uses a paired-run screen to select low-fidelity simulators for multi-fidelity simulation-based inference of epithelial vertex models. FSR networks use fixed-size Spearman regulons to enable uniform master-regulator analysis across 34 TCGA cohorts, benchmarked against ARACNe3 MI networks. A 20 kb-context regulatory sequence model reports that long-range information stops transferring between vertebrates before local sequence information does, revealing a phylogenetic boundary. Chimera applies a cross-species single-cell generative model to align atlases and score expression deployment against protein conservation. The items are heterogeneous in domain; the shared element is explicit attention to how model specification and comparison determine which biological conclusions are supported.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-15"
}
